W:1. A ploughman dresses ﬁne, he drinks strong beer, ale, and wine,
W:And the best of tobacco he do smoke.
W:"Pretty maids don't think amiss a ploughman for to kiss,
W:For his breath smells as sweet as a rose, a rose, a rose,
W:His breath smells as sweet as a rose."
W:
W:2. A ploughman in his shirt he completely does his work,
W:And so loudly to the little boy do call,
W:Saying: "Be nimble and be quick by the swishing of your whip."
W:And so merrily he'll rattle them along, along, along,
W:And so merrily he'll rattle them along.
W:
W:3. When our shears are shod, to the blacksmith off we wad,
W:And so loudly to the blacksmith we do call,
W:Saying: "Be nimble and be quick, and throw your blows in thick."
W:And so merrily he will swing his hammer round, around, around,
W:And so merrily he'll swing his hammer round.
W:
W:4. When our shears are clone, to the ale-house we will run,
W:And so loudly to the landlord we do call;
W:Saying: "Bring to us some beer, for while I am here,
W:A ploughman is always a-dry.
W:A ploughman is always a-dry, a-dry, a-dry."
